# CrackLib -- and the package is the DICTIONARY, not the library.
#
# WHY THAT DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE RECIPE. libpwquality's cracklib check calls
# FascistCheck(), which opens a compiled-in path and looks the candidate
# password up in it. If the dictionary at that path is absent, empty or
# truncated, FascistCheck does not error -- it returns "no problem", and
# gnome-control-center's Users panel draws a green strength bar saying the
# password was checked. Active false assurance: worse than having no check,
# because the interface asserts that a check happened.
#
# So the failure this recipe is written against is not "cracklib is missing" --
# that is loud, libpwquality's configure raises AC_MSG_ERROR. It is "cracklib is
# present and knows no words".
#
# THE PAYLOAD ARRIVES IN TWO PIECES, which is why fetch.sh grew a second source:
#   cracklib-2.10.3.tar.xz     456 KB   the library and its tools
#   cracklib-words-2.10.3.xz  4.16 MB   1,930,240 words (18.3 MB unpacked)
# Both digests are pinned; neither falls back. Sizes measured rather than
# quoted.
#
# BLFS 12.4, pinned here rather than in versions.env: versions.env is
# regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by tools/pin-versions.sh, so a desktop
# pin placed there is destroyed by the next `make pin`.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/cracklib/cracklib/releases/download/v2.10.3/cracklib-2.10.3.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=1d1b83676b5673051b9373e1ca0db81c1fa88faf9a195a3c1af984fd9c250d66
SRC_DIR=cracklib-2.10.3

# The word list, through the EXTRA_URL mechanism that already exists for
# exactly this -- glibc's FHS patch is its only other user today.
#
# NO NEW PLUMBING WAS NEEDED AND I NEARLY BUILT SOME. The first version of this
# recipe added a second source to _scripts/fetch.sh, because "fetch.sh takes
# exactly one SRC_URL" is true. It is also beside the point: tools/fetch-source.sh
# ALREADY fetches EXTRA_URL into the source cache (it has since glibc), the build
# container has no network by design, and the established shape is for the
# recipe's own stage to read the file out of $SRC_CACHE and re-verify it there.
# install.sh does exactly that, the same way pkgs/glibc/prepare.sh does.
EXTRA_URL=https://github.com/cracklib/cracklib/releases/download/v2.10.3/cracklib-words-2.10.3.xz
EXTRA_SHA256=b3d1e0e0ae9bc7ffed65c0042d7c68bdb903368aa6b918f94572453c6ea874dc

# --without-python IS EXPLICIT, AND THE BOOK IS THE REASON TO NOTICE IT.
# configure.ac:68-70 takes --with-python with default **auto**: it probes for a
# python and builds a module if it finds one. python IS packaged here, so the
# default would build a binding nothing in this tree consumes.
#
# BLFS's answer is not to turn it off but to feed it a path -- its command line
# is prefixed `CPPFLAGS+=' -I /usr/include/python3.13'`. That is a
# VERSION-PINNED include directory papering over an auto default: correct on the
# day it was written and silently wrong on the next python bump, which is the
# same class of decay as a hardcoded versioned path in an assertion. Turning the
# option off removes the need for the workaround entirely.
#
# --with-default-dict compiles the dictionary PATH into libcrack as a string
# (finding 42: a wrong compiled-in path is a path, not an error). It is stated
# here and asserted in install.sh against where the files actually landed --
# the p11-kit trust-path move -- because the flag and the outcome are two facts.
#
# --disable-static: as everywhere else in this tree.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-static \
--without-python \
--with-default-dict=/usr/lib/cracklib/pw_dict"
